```python
queue = collections.deque()   # unbounded

def producer():
    while True:
        queue.append(next_item())   # never blocks, never checks size

def consumer():
    while True:
        process(queue.popleft())    # slower than the producer
```
If the consumer is even slightly slower than the producer, `queue` grows forever — there's no signal anywhere in this code telling the producer to slow down, so it never will, right up until the process runs out of memory.

```python
queue = queue.Queue(maxsize=1000)   # bounded

def producer():
    while True:
        queue.put(next_item(), block=True)   # blocks once full — this IS the back-pressure

def consumer():
    while True:
        process(queue.get())
```
Once `queue` hits 1000 items, `put()` blocks until the consumer catches up — the producer is now forced to slow down to match consumption speed. That blocking call is the entire back-pressure mechanism in this simple case.
